{"kind":"assert","text":"Rulings on b3f879f6, the seven choices of cutover family plan 69fed843.\nI read plan.md of v2 in full. These are scope rulings. They allocate no\nversion and authorize no source edit before identifiers (c+d) is in\nreview.\n\n1. Placement. A separate head after (c+d) and before the cutover\n   deployment. I hold that deployment until the family is on main, so\n   each room is recreated once.\n\n2. Leaving. Both acts enter the new Membership.\n   a. Own key. An active key retires itself or marks itself\n      compromised through `revoke-key`, as the plan words it. Refusal\n      `not-own-key`.\n   b. Own membership. A new act `leave`, signed by an active key of the\n      member that it names. Every member kind may use it: person, agent\n      and checker. Its one effect is `removed`. Refusal\n      `not-own-member`. It adds no client screen in this head.\n   c. Last admin. In the new Membership, the guard `last-admin-kept`\n      passes without its check only for proved recovery authorization.\n      A null grant alone no longer passes it. One condition then covers\n      the self branch, the controller branch, `leave` and any later\n      branch. The administrator grant and the recovery route keep their\n      behavior. Witness the case that the peer review found: a grant\n      mark that returns a member with a null grant still meets the\n      guard.\n   d. The hosted retire keeps ruling e2fc59d5. The endpoint erases the\n      seed and never signs. Branch 3b below admits the envelope of an\n      ordinary controller, so that retire now completes.\n\n3. Several keys.\n   a. B = 4, invited and active keys counted together. The 10,000 key\n      items and the eight controlled agents keep their bounds.\n   b. An active person revokes an active key of an agent that they\n      control: exact controller, exact agent, no role-subset check.\n      Admins and recovery keep their route.\n   c. No early cancellation of an invited key. It lapses within 24\n      hours and counts until its lapse is recorded. Membership has no\n      cancel act today, and this family adds none.\n\n4. Publication paths.\n   a. Encoding and bounds as proposed: `publication-paths@1` with one\n      to eight literal directory prefixes, at most 256 UTF-8 bytes\n      each, one trailing slash, compared by path segment. No glob, root,\n      absolute path, dot segment, `.git` path or reserved prefix.\n   b. No declaration means no `publish-file` authority. A package with\n      no declaration still installs.\n   c. Claims are exclusive within a Directory. Overlap with a pending\n      or created application refuses `publication-prefix-in-use`. Only\n      a recorded failed creation releases a pending claim. A created\n      application keeps its claim. This family has no sharing and no\n      reassignment.\n   d. A definition activated without a package gains no `publish-file`\n      authority in the new family, and no other declaration route.\n   e. The four capability sets are exactly: none, publication, device,\n      and device with publication.\n\n5. Carrier and placement.\n   a. One new closed marker in the creation context carries the\n      approved prefixes, with the same source, seed and Directory proof\n      as the device grant. You choose its shape. Add no unrecognized\n      field to an application's declared genesis.\n   b. Split the check in two, and add no new evidence type to the\n      Native judge. The new Destination judges the requested path\n      against the proved prefixes. The path and the prefixes stand in\n      the record, so replay derives `publication-path-not-declared`.\n      The alias check stays in the host, where\n      `publicationRefusal` in packages/scope/src/publication-policy.ts\n      reads `JudgeChanges` today. Extend that function: after the\n      reserved-path check, every touched path and link target must\n      stand inside the declared prefixes. The host refuses before the\n      push, with the not-sent evidence that it records for a reserved\n      path today.\n   c. If the host placement cannot carry the alias refusal, report the\n      exact gap before you add evidence to the judge.\n\n6. The stable default. One family composes: the failed-claim final\n   state, the separation of founder and recovery, the fixed one-hour\n   token of Destination 19, eight controlled agents, archive binding\n   and repository binding. No regression of one of these is acceptable.\n   The witness \"Whole family\" of your table covers it. Leave Commons\n   reference installs out. Commons keeps its own Rules line, and the\n   new Rules pin refuses reference fields by an explicit choice, not by\n   its number.\n\n7. Versions. Allocate names and digests only from the actual (c+d)\n   source. Reuse the Inbox pin of (c+d) unless that head changes it.\n\nAlso in this head, from assert 710a5d76: the ordinary version 2\npackage beside the device command, with the Counter witness through\n`dap pack` and the device witness that you list.\n\nNot in this head: publisher-key trust (the owner decides), the archive\ndriver, team objects, a cancel act, and client screens for `leave`.\nSix reader pages say that no room of a current Worker installs an\nordinary package. This head removes that sentence. docs/cli.md\n\"Creation cohorts\" is the one source.\n\nReport the shared paths with G, H, JP1 and OPS-2 before source edits.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}